<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pepin of Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">777–810</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Pippin of Italy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pepin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, born Carloman, who was crowned king of Italy in 781 to rule the conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kingdom. He campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. In 810 he attacked the Venetian lagoon, and his failure to take the islands helped move the seat of Venetian government to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rialto">Rialto</a>. He died later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy.jpg" alt="Pepin of Italy" width="279" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%A9pin_d%27Italie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
